          "case_name": "DeShaney v. Winnebago County Department of Social Services",
          "citation": "489 U.S. 189 (1989)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of the United States",
          "year": 1989,
          "date_decided": "1989-02-22",
          "holding": "The Court held that the Due Process Clause imposes no affirmative constitutional duty on a State to protect a child from violence by a private actor, even where the agency knew of the danger and had intervened, absent a custodial restraint on the child's liberty.",
          "relevance": "Agency knowledge of danger, without state custody, does not create the special relationship needed for § 1983 liability.",
          "quote": "its language cannot fairly be extended to impose an affirmative obligation on the State to ensure that those interests do not come to harm through other means",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112202/deshaney-v-winnebago-county-department-of-social-services/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 112202
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Joshua M. v. State",
          "citation": "316 Neb. 446 (2024)",
          "court": "Nebraska Supreme Court",
          "year": 2024,
          "date_decided": "2024-05-03",
          "holding": "The court held that the siblings' negligence claims against DHHS arising from years of sexual and physical abuse by their foster father fall within the State Tort Claims Act's exemption for claims arising out of assault or battery, so sovereign immunity was not waived and the district court lacked subject matter jurisdiction.",
          "relevance": "Children abused while under agency supervision have no damages remedy against DHHS in Nebraska where the harm arises out of assault or battery.",
          "quote": "because we conclude the court lacked subject matter jurisdiction over the siblings' claims against DHHS, we do not reach the merits of any assigned error",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/9999371/joshua-m-v-state/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 9999371
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Jill B. & Travis B. v. State",
          "citation": "297 Neb. 57 (2017)",
          "court": "Nebraska Supreme Court",
          "year": 2017,
          "date_decided": "2017-06-30",
          "holding": "The court held that foster and adoptive parents' claims that the State failed to disclose the placed child's sexual history are barred by the State Tort Claims Act's misrepresentation exception, including any theory framed as an independent operational duty to disclose or warn.",
          "relevance": "It is the companion limitation to Joshua M. on agency liability for harm to children and caregivers in the foster system.",
          "quote": "Any operational duty to disclose information or to warn the parents about K.D.M.'s sexual history is subsumed by the misrepresentation exception.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4428185/jill-b-travis-b-v-state/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 4428185
        }
      ],
